e who are going to be  affected their families are going to be  affected as well nobody goes on strike  lightly but as you say in west yorkshire  we have a dispute with arriva and unite  and i am hoping that over the next day  or so we could get to some resolution to  make sure we can see the end of this  it's really important that working  people can say to government that um we  are seeing a real uh cut in our wages  due to inflation and my understanding is  that railway workers haven't had a pay  rise in over two to three years and it  is fair and just that they can make  their case uh mayor braven it sounds to  me like you support these strikes  well i support passengers  i am i am the voice of the passenger i  am a boss untrained passenger myself  what i what i support is the right to  strike that is the last resort for  people who want to see better paying  conditions but of course the disruption  is great uh as we've seen in wakefield  it's been really hard for that community  because we don't have a a fantastic rail  network in wakefield and and the five  towns  the community really rely on the buses  there are people paying 20 pounds a day  in taxes to get to work a hundred pounds  a week and it's been going on now into  the third week this cannot continue and  we have to come to some sort of question  then if you support the right to strike  the strikes are causing disruption for  the people you represent in west  yorkshire  and of course people right across the  country what is the solution if not a  pay rise and better working conditions  well that's absolutely absolutely the  solution is coming to a compromise where  people are paid properly for a day's  work and also that um we don't see those  cuts to numbers in in so-called  efficiencies and protecting people's  right to have a a safe and decent  environment to work in it is down to  those companies like ariva and also i  would suggest the governments to to get  involved in in in finding a solution
 

